# 23. PRODUCT-LEVEL FUNCTIONALITY

The following end-to-end workflows must actually work.

## Workflow A — Citizen pickup request

1. Citizen registers.
2. Citizen logs in.
3. Citizen adds household details.
4. Citizen opens pickup request.
5. Citizen selects waste category.
6. Citizen selects preferred date and time.
7. Citizen submits the request.
8. Backend validates the request.
9. Database stores the request.
10. Citizen receives a request reference number.
11. Supervisor sees the new request.

## Workflow B — Supervisor scheduling

1. Supervisor logs in.
2. Opens pending pickup requests.
3. Reviews request details.
4. Approves the request.
5. Selects collection date.
6. Selects time slot.
7. Assigns an active collector.
8. System saves the assignment.
9. Citizen sees the updated status.
10. Collector sees the assigned pickup.

## Workflow C — Collector completion

1. Collector logs in.
2. Opens assigned pickups.
3. Views address and waste details.
4. Starts the collection.
5. Updates status to In Progress.
6. Completes the collection.
7. Records collected quantity and remarks.
8. Backend saves the status history.
9. Citizen sees Completed status.
10. Dashboard statistics update.

## Workflow D — Missed collection

1. Collector cannot complete a pickup.
2. Selects Mark as Missed.
3. Selects a reason.
4. Adds remarks.
5. Submits the update.
6. Supervisor sees the missed pickup.
7. Supervisor reschedules or reassigns it.
8. Citizen receives an updated status.

## Workflow E — Reports

1. Admin opens Reports.
2. Selects a report.
3. Applies filters.
4. Backend queries PostgreSQL.
5. Frontend displays results.
6. Admin exports CSV or prints the report.

Do not simulate these workflows using local frontend state alone.

All persistent changes must go through backend APIs.

